For any $x, y, z \neq 0$ with $xyz=1$, we have three equations by cyclically permuting the variables:
\begin{align*}
f(x)^2 - f(y)f(z) &= x(x+y+z)(f(x)+f(y)+f(z)), \\
f(y)^2 - f(z)f(x) &= y(x+y+z)(f(x)+f(y)+f(z)), \\
f(z)^2 - f(x)f(y) &= z(x+y+z)(f(x)+f(y)+f(z)).
\end{align*}

We verify that $f(x)=x^2 - \frac{1}{x}$ satisfies the original equation. Let $xyz=1$. Compute:
\begin{align*}
f(x)^2 - f(y)f(z) &= x^4 - 2x + \frac{1}{x^2} - \left(\frac{1}{x^2} - x(y^3+z^3) + x\right) = x^4 - 3x + x(y^3+z^3), \\
x(x+y+z)(f(x)+f(y)+f(z)) &= xT\left(T^2 - 3(xy+yz+zx)\right) = xT^3 - 3xT(xy+yz+zx).
\end{align*}
Using $T^3 = x^3+y^3+z^3 + 3T(xy+yz+zx) - 3xyz = x^3+y^3+z^3 + 3T(xy+yz+zx) - 3$, the right side becomes $x(x^3+y^3+z^3 - 3)$. Since $x^4 - 3x + x(y^3+z^3) = x(x^3+y^3+z^3 - 3)$, equality holds.

